!
! Line 5's SLC,$ prints a LOCATION and no binary, which is what an
! SLC does: it emits nothing, so the location it prints IS the
! counter arriving from line 4 (221733.00 plus one half word).  Line
! 6 then prints the same 221733.40.
!

!SYM KUNLOD 221733.00
!SYM ZUNLO1 221740.00
!SYM ZUNLO3 221741.00
!SYM KFUNLD 221741.40
!SYM KRITET 221743.00
!SYM KUNLD1 221751.00
!SYM KFIRE  221756.00
!SYM KNEWTP 221766.00
!
!CHECK 221733.00 | 222017.44 30 | line 4 K UNLOD LV,2,K UL MES -- KULMES is landed at 222017.40 by printed p.184 and p.197's OCR line 26 defines it there; LV puts 2J = 4 in the low field and bit 32 as 0o40, so 0o44 checks the half word and the index together
!CHECK 221733.40 | 000000.63 8B 001000.36 F0 | line 6 CM1111(BU,1),SMOUNT($11) -- a VFL operand reads every bit, so 0o63 = bit 51 CHECKS the landed SMOUNT exactly; 8B is the $11 index and 001000.36 F0 is the same CM1111(BU,1) word printed p.18 prints six times
!CHECK 221734.40 | 222001.13 01 | line 7 LVI,5,K UNL M -- 0o13 is 2J+1 for J=5 with bit 32 CLEAR, which is what checks the landed KUNLM at 222001.0; byte 01 is the bare LVI opcode
!CHECK 221735.00 | 000000.40 8B 221740.34 02 | line 8 BB,S OWNER($11),Z UNLO1 -- BB's first operand is a 24-bit BIT address, so 0o40 = bit 32 CHECKS the landed SOWNER to the bit; the branch half carries THIS page's ZUNLO1 at 221740.00 with the BB code 0o34
!CHECK 221736.00 | 222011.17 01 | line 9 LVI,7,Z UNL O2 -- 0o17 is 2J+1 for J=7, bit 32 clear, checking the landed ZUNLO2 at 222011.0
!CHECK 221736.40 | 221045.00 80 | line 10 SIC,Z ID09X -- A SIC CARRIES THE FULL 24-BIT BIT ADDRESS, so this row READS ZID09X at 221045.00 and therefore CHECKS the value four landed decks carry; same card as printed p.179 line 47 and printed p.184 line 12
!CHECK 221737.00 | 221042.10 00 | line 11 B,Z ID09 -CONVERT NAME -- a plain B shows bit 32 only (0o00 OR the B code 0o10); same card as printed p.179 line 48 and printed p.184 line 13
!CHECK 221737.40 | 221741.10 00 | line 12 B,Z UNL O3 -- ZUNLO3 is THIS page's line 14 at 221741.00, so this row pins the label
!CHECK 221740.00 | 221062.00 80 222011.02 A0 | line 13 Z UNLO1 TI,1,Z ID16,Z UNLO2 -- ZID16 is printed p.180's line 22 at 221062.00; the second half is ZUNLO2 with 2J = 2 for J=1, so the row checks the landed external and the index encoding together
!CHECK 221741.00 | 000011.17 01 | line 14 Z UNLO3 LVI,7,9. -- a bare immediate: 9 decimal prints as 0o11 in the address field, and 0o17 is 2J+1 for J=7
!CHECK 221741.40 | 000010.62 3C | line 15 K F UNLD LV,9,S C REEL($12) -- 0o62 is bit 32 (0o40) OR 2J = 18 = 0o22 for J=9, which checks the landed SCREEL at 8.32; byte 3C is the LV opcode 30 OR the $12 index C
!CHECK 221742.00 | 000000.20 39 | line 16 LV,8,0.0($9) -ADDR OF NEXT REEL NAME -- 0o20 is 2J for J=8 with bit 32 clear; byte 39 is LV OR the $9 index
!CHECK 221742.40 | 221766.71 42 | line 17 BXVZ,KCUSTM -IF SCRATCH NEEDED -- BXVZ is a BRANCH, so only bit 32 of the operand shows: measured, KCUSTM at .0 prints 221766.31 and at .40 prints 221766.71, so the .40 IS read and the bits below it are convention.  printed p.197's OCR line 1 defines K CUSTM at 221766.40
!CHECK 221743.00 | 000000.42 88 036600.06 70 | line 18 K RITE T LF(BU,30,6),.34($8) -IF NOT, GET NEW REEL NO -- the bare .34 is DECIMAL 34 and prints 0o42; the second half carries length 30 = 0o36 and byte size 6 = 0o600 with the LF code 06 70.  printed p.184 line 28 prints the identical second word for the same statement with a different index
!CHECK 221744.00 | 221775.34 C2 | line 19 BRZ,KKTAPE -- 0o34 is the BRZ code and bit 32 is clear; printed p.197's OCR line 11 defines K K TAPE at 221775.00
!CHECK 221744.40 | 222015.00 80 222006.04 A0 | line 20 TI,2,K EX TAP,K REEL N -- the first half is the landed KEXTAP at 222015.00, the second is KREELN with 2J = 4 for J=2.  printed p.197's OCR line 11 prints this same second half word for "TI,2,K SCRAC,K REEL N"
!CHECK 221745.40 | 222007.10 80 050000.12 F0 | line 21 SF(BU,40),KREELN+.72 -- A VFL OPERAND READS EVERY BIT, so KREELN + 72 DECIMAL bits printing 222007.10 pins KREELN at 222006.00 outright; the second half carries length 40 = 0o50 and the connective code F0
!CHECK 221746.40 | 221747.77 01 | line 22 LVI,15,$+1.0 -- $ is this row's 221746.40 so $+1.0 is 221747.40, bit 32 SET; 0o77 is 0o40 OR 2J+1 = 31 = 0o37 for J=15.  This row carries the second location-column asterisk
!CHECK 221747.00 | 230566.10 00 | line 23 B,S A8 IQS -- printed p.147's landed deck SYNs SA8IQS at 230566.0 and its line 39 prints this identical half word; bit offset unobservable
!CHECK 221747.40 | 222007.10+ | line 24 VF,K REEL N+.72 -- A VF DEPOSITS THE VALUE, so this row is a BIT-EXACT reading of KREELN: 222007.08 minus 72 decimal bits is 222006.00.  Read .72 as octal and it lands at 222007.26 instead, which is how the decimal-bare rule gets checked here
!CHECK 221750.00 | 000005 | line 25 CF,5 -- an 18-bit COUNT field, printed as six bare octal digits with no bit position and no hex byte
!CHECK 221750.40 | 222007.10+ | line 26 VF,K REEL N+.72 -- the SAME card as line 24, printed a second time on one page; two identical words from two cards is the page's own check on the VF encoding
!CHECK 221751.00 | 000000.32 88 221752.74 00 | line 27 K UNLD1 BZB,.26($8),$+1.32 -TEST IF PROTECTED -- the bare .26 is DECIMAL 26 and prints 0o32; $+1.32 from 221751.00 is 221752.40, so 0o74 is 0o40 OR the BZB code 0o34.  printed p.184 SYNs KUNLD1 at 221751.0
!CHECK 221752.00 | 000012.17 01 | line 28 LVI,7,10. -FILE PROTECT MESSAGE LENGTH -- 10 decimal prints 0o12, and 0o17 is 2J+1 for J=7
!CHECK 221752.40 | 221754.05 30 | line 29 SV,2,$+1.32 -- $+1.32 from 221752.40 is 221754.00, bit 32 CLEAR; 0o05 is 2J+1 for J=2, which is how SV is distinguished from LV, whose byte 30 it shares (line 4 prints 2J for the same byte)
!CHECK 221753.00 | 221350.00 80 | line 30 SIC,K CONV R -- a SIC reads every bit, so this CHECKS the landed KCONVR at 221350.00
!CHECK 221753.40 | 221335.10 00 | line 31 B,K CONV -- the landed KCONV at 221335.0; bit offset unobservable
!CHECK 221754.00 | 000000.00+ | line 32 VF,0.0 -- the argument word of the K CONV call
!CHECK 221754.40 | 000000.30 00 | line 33 CNOP -- the counter is at .40 here, so the CNOP EMITS a half word and prints one; printed p.192 line 14 is the opposite case, a CNOP at a word boundary that prints nothing at all
!CHECK 221755.00 | 221756.00 10 | line 34 LX,0,$+1.0 -- $+1.0 from 221755.00 is 221756.00; 0o00 is 2J for J=0 and the byte is the bare LX opcode, NOT 1C -- line 35 is the indexed case
!CHECK 221755.40 | 000004.01 1C | line 35 SX,0,S IO INS($12) -- the landed SIOINS at 4.0; 0o01 is 2J+1 for J=0, which is the store bit, and byte 1C is the same 10 as line 34 OR the $12 index
!CHECK 221756.00 | 000000.00 8E 000137.15 00 | line 36 K FIRE -- THE PAGE PRINTS "UNLOAD(SEOP),0.0($14)" AND strap_asm REFUSES IT; the deck writes CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),(8)137. which compiles this exact word.  8E's low nibble is the $14 index, .15 is the CTLSEOP code, 0o137 is the Rewind-and-Unload control code of Reference Manual p.132.  See THE UNLOAD PROBLEM
!CHECK 221757.00 | 221760.03 01 | line 37 LVI,1,$+1.0 -- $+1.0 from 221757.00 is 221760.00; 0o03 is 2J+1 for J=1
!CHECK 221757.40 | 221261.10 00 | line 38 B,M IO REJ -- the landed MIOREJ at 221261.0; bit offset unobservable
!CHECK 221760.00 | 221354.40 80 | line 39 SIC,K EOP R -- a SIC reads every bit, so this CHECKS the landed KEOPR at 221354.40
!CHECK 221760.40 | 221350.50 00 | line 40 B,K SEOP T -- the landed KSEOPT at 221350.40: 0o40 OR the B code 0o10 is 0o50, so bit 32 IS checked.  Note that KSEOPT and KCONVR (line 30) are the two halves of the SAME WORD 221350
!CHECK 221761.00 | 215470.40 80 | line 41 SIC,S COMIC -- a SIC reads every bit, so this CHECKS the landed SCOMIC at 215470.40
!CHECK 221761.40 | 215470.10 00 | line 42 B,S COMM -- the landed SCOMM at 215470.0, the other half of word 215470
!CHECK 221762.00 | 000000.00 85 | line 43 ,0.0($5) -- A BLANK OPERATION FIELD, written as printed; byte 85 is the $5 index on an otherwise empty half word.  This row carries the third location-column asterisk
!CHECK 221762.40 | 000000.00 87 | line 44 ,0.0($7) -- the same shape with $7; two such cards in a row is what makes them an argument list rather than a misread mnemonic
!CHECK 221763.00 | 000001.34 80 032000.20 50 | line 45 L(BU,26),$TC -SET TIME FOR REMINDER MESSAGE -- $TC is the system time clock at word 1 bit 28 = 0o34, read bit-exactly because L is VFL; the second half is length 26 = 0o32 with the load byte 50
!CHECK 221764.00 | 000001.40 8C 032000.20 D0 | line 46 ST,S RMTC($12) -- the landed SRMTC at 1.32 prints 000001.40 bit-exactly (ST is VFL); THE LENGTH 26 IS INHERITED FROM THE SYMBOL because the statement writes no dds, so this row is the check on "SRMTC SYN(BU,26)" and fails outright if that dds is dropped
!CHECK 221765.00 | 000001.77 8C 001000.00 F0 | line 47 CM0000,S RMIND($12) -CLEAR INDICATOR -- the landed SRMIND at 1.63 prints 000001.77 bit-exactly; the length 1 is inherited the same way, so this row checks "SRMIND SYN(BU,1)" as well as the address
!CHECK 221766.00 | 216242.10 00 | line 48 K NEW TP B,K SUPP -- the landed KSUPP at 216242.0, which thirty-seven decks carry; bit offset unobservable
